    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.directed_steiner_tree - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.directed_steiner_tree.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.directed_steiner_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find an approximate directed Steiner tree (Steiner arborescence).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.directed_steiner_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Find an approximate directed Steiner tree (Steiner arborescence).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.directed_steiner_tree()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find an approximate directed Steiner tree (Steiner arborescence).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind an approximate directed Steiner tree (Steiner arborescence).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L79728-L79857){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.directed_steiner_tree(\n    root,\n    terminal_nodes,\n    weight_attr=None,\n    return_subgraph=True,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Directed Steiner tree](../assets/graphs/directed-steiner-tree.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"A directed version of the steiner_tree graph, rooted at B with terminals C and E outlined. The edge between C and D now points from C to D, so C cannot be reached through D: the arborescence B→D→E→C (accent) reaches E through the non-terminal D and then C from E. The card lists the three edges and their total weight 2 + 1 + 4 = 7.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nGiven a root node and a set of terminal nodes, finds an approximate minimum-weight\narborescence rooted at `root` containing a directed path from `root` to every terminal.\nOther (non-terminal) nodes are included only when they help reach a terminal cheaply.\nThis is the directed counterpart of `steiner_tree()`: every edge must be followed in its\nown direction.\n\nUses the Shortest-Path Heuristic (SPH) with edge-cost reuse: greedily selects the cheapest\npath from root to an uncovered terminal, then zeroes the cost of used edges so future paths\nreuse them for free. This encourages path sharing and produces compact arborescences. Any\nterminal that lies on a chosen path is covered along the way. The paths are then improved:\nthe cheapest arborescence from the root over the nodes they reach (by Edmonds' algorithm,\non every edge among them) replaces them when it costs less, with the branches that lead to\nno terminal pruned away. This catches a terminal that is cheaper to reach from a node\nthat a later path brought in.\n\nTypical uses include:\n\n- multicast routing: delivering a stream from one source to a set of receivers\n- planning one-way distribution networks (pipelines, supply chains) from a single hub\n- finding the cheapest set of directed dependencies or signaling paths that link a\n  source to a set of targets of interest\n\nThe result is a heuristic and is not guaranteed to be optimal (the exact problem is\nNP-hard). Edge weights should be non-negative. When there are parallel edges between two\nnodes, the cheapest one is used.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[root]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The root (source) node, as an integer ID or a string label. Every path in the\n    arborescence starts here.\n\n<code>[terminal_nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list[int | str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   List of the nodes (IDs or labels) that must be reachable from `root=`. At least one\n    is required. Duplicates are ignored, and the root itself is skipped if it is listed.\n\n<code>[weight_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Name of the edge attribute to use as edge cost. If `None` (the default), each edge's\n    weight field is used (or `1.0` on an unweighted graph, so the tree minimizes the\n    number of edges), as it is for `\"weight\"`. Under any other name, every edge must hold\n    a number.\n\n<code>[return_subgraph]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[True]{.va}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   If `True` (the default), return a new directed `Graph` containing only the nodes and\n    edges of the arborescence. If `False`, return its edges as\n    `(source_id, target_id, weight)` tuples instead.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [Graph or list[tuple[int, int, float]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `return_subgraph=` is `True`: a new directed `Graph` holding the arborescence's\n    nodes and edges, with the original weighting and their original IDs, labels, weights\n    and attributes. Otherwise a list of `(source_id, target_id, weight)` tuples (weight\n    being the edge's cost) sorted by source and then target ID.\n    If the only terminal is the root, the result is empty.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is not directed, `terminal_nodes=` is empty, the root or a terminal is not\n    found, a terminal is not reachable from the root, or `weight_attr=` names an attribute\n    that an edge lacks or holds as a non-number.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~steiner_tree`) \n\n:   The same, ignoring edge directions.\n\n[](`~minimum_spanning_arborescence`) \n\n:   The cheapest arborescence that reaches every node.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nFrom `R`, the tree reaches `B` and `D` by sharing the edge into `A`, rather than taking\nthe expensive edge straight to `C`:\n\n::: {#71bdde43 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"R\", \"A\", 1.0), (\"A\", \"B\", 2.0), (\"R\", \"C\", 5.0),\n     (\"A\", \"C\", 1.0), (\"C\", \"D\", 1.0)],\n    directed=True,\n)\ngraph.directed_steiner_tree(\"R\", [\"B\", \"D\"]).get_edges_str_repr(\n    use_labels=True\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['R->A', 'A->B', 'A->C', 'C->D']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Supplying two districts\n\n`pipeline()` is a made-up city's water supply, with each pipe's `capacity` in megaliters\na day and its running `cost`. Keeping water flowing from the Reservoir to the North and\nSouth Districts alone costs 8, with the two routes sharing the pipes as far as Valley\nJunction:\n\n::: {#313f305e .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline = ec.pipeline()\ndistricts = [\"North District\", \"South District\"]\ntree = pipeline.directed_steiner_tree(\n    \"Reservoir\", districts, weight_attr=\"cost\"\n)\n\ntree.get_edge_df()[\"cost\"].sum()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n8\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#8077a937 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\npipeline.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    highlight={\"accent\": [\"Reservoir\"] + districts},\n    highlight_edges={\"soft\": tree.get_edge_ids()},\n    label_by=\"label\",\n    width=700,\n    height=360,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-9f8a22df:#efc5b7;--ect-6c2f7015:#a78980;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-9f8a22df:#4c2e23;--ect-6c2f7015:#352018;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-9f8a22df:#efc5b7;--ect-6c2f7015:#a78980;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425